Use a world-space Unity UI board with Meta Interaction SDK ray support. Your existing CD_Control component already
  exposes the ratio fields, so the board only needs to call setters.

  1. Create The Script
  Create Assets/C#/CDRatioBoard.cs:

  using TMPro;
  using UnityEngine;
  using UnityEngine.UI;

  public class CDRatioBoard : MonoBehaviour
  {
      [SerializeField] private CD_Control cdControl;
      [SerializeField] private TMP_Text leftValueText;
      [SerializeField] private TMP_Text rightValueText;
      [SerializeField] private Color selectedColor = new Color(0.2f, 0.8f, 1f);
      [SerializeField] private Color normalColor = Color.white;

      private Button[] leftButtons;
      private Button[] rightButtons;

      void Start()
      {
          leftButtons = transform.Find("LeftColumn").GetComponentsInChildren<Button>();
          rightButtons = transform.Find("RightColumn").GetComponentsInChildren<Button>();
          Refresh();
      }

      public void SetLeftRatio(float ratio)
      {
          cdControl.leftArmCDRatio = ratio;
          Refresh();
      }

      public void SetRightRatio(float ratio)
      {
          cdControl.rightArmCDRatio = ratio;
          Refresh();
      }

      public void Recalibrate()
      {
          cdControl.RequestCalibration();
      }

      void Refresh()
      {
          leftValueText.text = $"Left: {cdControl.leftArmCDRatio:0.0}";
          rightValueText.text = $"Right: {cdControl.rightArmCDRatio:0.0}";

          UpdateColors(leftButtons, cdControl.leftArmCDRatio);
          UpdateColors(rightButtons, cdControl.rightArmCDRatio);
      }

      void UpdateColors(Button[] buttons, float selectedRatio)
      {
          foreach (Button button in buttons)
          {
              float ratio = float.Parse(button.GetComponentInChildren<TMP_Text>().text);
              ColorBlock colors = button.colors;
              colors.normalColor = Mathf.Approximately(ratio, selectedRatio)
                  ? selectedColor
                  : normalColor;
              button.colors = colors;
          }
      }
  }

  2. Create The Board

  1. Open Assets/Scenes/Pseudo Haptic.unity.
  2. Right-click Hierarchy.
  3. Select UI > Canvas.
  4. Rename it CDRatioBoard.
  5. Set the Canvas Render Mode to World Space.
  6. Set its Rect Transform:
      - Width: 900
      - Height: 600
      - Scale: 0.002, 0.002, 0.002

  7. Position it approximately two meters in front of the starting position, around chest height.
  8. Rotate it so its front faces the player.

  Add a UI > Panel under the canvas as the background. Use a dark, mostly opaque color.

  3. Add Titles
  Add TextMeshPro text objects:

  C/D Ratio Control
  Left Arm
  Right Arm
  Left: 1.0
  Right: 1.0

  Name the last two:

  - LeftValueText
  - RightValueText

  4. Create Columns

  1. Create two empty Rect Transform objects under CDRatioBoard.
  2. Name them exactly:
      - LeftColumn
      - RightColumn

  3. Add Vertical Layout Group to each column.
  4. Under each column, create six UI > Button - TextMeshPro objects.
  5. Set their visible labels to:

  0.3
  0.6
  1.0
  1.3
  1.6
  2.0

  Make each button large enough for easy VR targeting, such as 180 x 55.

  5. Add The Board Controller

  1. Add CDRatioBoard to the root CDRatioBoard Canvas object.
  2. Drag your avatar’s existing CD_Control component into Cd Control.
  3. Drag LeftValueText and RightValueText into their matching fields.

  6. Connect Each Button
  For every button:

  1. In the Inspector, find Button > On Click().
  2. Press +.
  3. Drag the root CDRatioBoard Canvas into the object field.
  4. For left-column buttons, select:
     CDRatioBoard > SetLeftRatio(float)

  5. For right-column buttons, select:
     CDRatioBoard > SetRightRatio(float)

  6. Type the matching number into the float field.

  For example, the left 0.6 button calls SetLeftRatio(0.6).

  7. Add Recalibration
  Add one more button named RecalibrateButton with visible text:

  Recalibrate Neutral Pose

  Connect its On Click() event to:

  CDRatioBoard > Recalibrate()

  Stand naturally before pressing it.

  8. Enable VR Clicking
  Your installed Meta SDK includes PointableCanvasModule.

  1. Select the scene’s EventSystem.
  2. Add component Pointable Canvas Module.
  3. Remove or disable Standalone Input Module if both modules compete for input.
  4. Select the Canvas.
  5. Use Meta XR Interaction SDK’s Ray Canvas quick-action wizard if it appears in the Inspector or Meta XR menu.
  6. Confirm your rig contains active RayInteractor components for controller rays or hand rays.

  Your current scene already contains Meta interaction rig assets, so this should reuse them.

  9. Test

  1. Enter Play Mode with Quest Link first.
  2. Point a controller ray at a button.
  3. Click using the trigger.
  4. Confirm the selected button changes color and the displayed ratio changes.
  5. Build the APK and repeat on Quest 3.

  Start with 1.0 on both arms. Then test one arm at 0.6 and the other at 1.6 to make the difference obvious.

  Meta reference: OVRBody class (https://developers.meta.com/horizon/reference/unity/v69/class_o_v_r_body)
